# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GLOBIN code owners.
#
# THIS IS THE ONLY CODEOWNERS FILE. GitHub also honours one at the repository
# root and one under `docs/`, and it uses whichever it finds first rather than
# merging them, so a second copy would silently override this one. A governance
# check refuses a duplicate for that reason, not for tidiness.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE DOES HERE. The repository is public (ADR-0046), so a pull
# request can now arrive from anybody. When one does, GitHub requests review
# from the owner of each path it touches. That is a real mechanism and it is
# why this file exists.
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT DO. It does not enforce review. Enforcement would need a
# ruleset rule requiring code-owner approval, and that is NOT_APPLICABLE here
# rather than merely unset: GLOBIN develops on `master` with no pull request
# (ADR-0005), and GitHub does not let anybody approve their own pull request, so
# a sole maintainer requiring their own approval would be unable to merge
# anything. The reasoning is recorded once, in
# `docs/security/GOVERNANCE.md`, and the state is recorded in the governance
# manifest rather than reported as a passing control.
#
# ONE OWNER, AND NO INVENTED ONES. `aydhn` owns this repository as a personal
# account, not an organisation, so `@org/team` syntax cannot resolve here and
# none is written. An owner GitHub cannot resolve is ignored silently, which
# turns a governance file into decoration.
#
# ORDER MATTERS: the LAST matching pattern wins. The catch-all is therefore
# first, and every security-sensitive path is listed after it. Those entries are
# redundant for *assignment* — the catch-all already names the same owner — and
# they are not redundant as a declaration. They are the list of paths a change
# to which is security-sensitive, they are compared against
# `docs/engineering/governance.toml` by the governance gate, and they will still
# be right on the day this repository has a second maintainer and the catch-all
# stops being the answer.
#
# Every pattern below matches something that exists. A pattern matching nothing
# is not a harmless precaution: it reads as coverage while providing none, and
# the governance gate fails on one.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Everything, unless a later line is more specific.
*                               @aydhn

# --- Continuous integration: the only place this repository executes code it
# --- did not write, and the only holder of a token.
/.github/workflows/             @aydhn
/.github/dependabot.yml         @aydhn
/.pre-commit-config.yaml        @aydhn

# --- Governance itself. A change here changes who must look at everything else.
/.github/CODEOWNERS             @aydhn
/SECURITY.md                    @aydhn
/.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/        @aydhn
/.github/pull_request_template.md  @aydhn

# --- The security and supply-chain contracts.
/docs/security/                 @aydhn
/docs/DEPENDENCY_POLICY.md      @aydhn
/docs/engineering/CI_SECURITY.md         @aydhn
/docs/engineering/action-pins.toml       @aydhn
/docs/engineering/dependency-reviews.toml @aydhn
/docs/engineering/vulnerability-waivers.toml @aydhn
/docs/engineering/governance.toml        @aydhn

# --- The gates. Tooling that decides whether a commit is acceptable, plus the
# --- one settings file that configures all of it.
/tools/                         @aydhn
/scripts/                       @aydhn
/pyproject.toml                 @aydhn

# --- Decisions and binding instructions.
/docs/adr/                      @aydhn
/AGENTS.md                      @aydhn
